# Scale Expectations

Expected scale affects architecture and performance tradeoffs. Over-engineering is waste; under-engineering is crisis.

## Options

ScaleLevels {
  SingleUser => Personal tool, one person
  SmallGroup => Team, family, < 100 users
  Organization => Company-wide, 100-10K
  LargeScale => Popular app, 10K-1M
  InternetScale => Massive traffic, 1M+
}

## Derived Architecture

| Scale | Database | Caching | Architecture | Async |
|-------|----------|---------|--------------|-------|
| Single | SQLite/file | None | Monolith | UX only |
| Small | Single DB | Optional | Monolith | Long ops |
| Org | Optimized | Redis | Simple services | I/O bound |
| Large | Replicas | Required | Services | Pervasive |
| Internet | Sharded | Multi-layer | Microservices | Everything |

## AI Behavior Calibration

BehaviorsByScale {
  SingleUser => ignore scalability, in-memory fine, SQLite fine, simplicity over performance
  SmallGroup => basic performance awareness, simple queries, skip horizontal scaling
  Organization => consider query performance, indexing, caching, connection pooling
  LargeScale => performance-conscious, DB optimization, rate limiting, monitoring
  InternetScale => performance-first, distributed patterns, eventual consistency, CDN
}

## Scale × Lifecycle

| | PoC | MVP | Production |
|---|-----|-----|------------|
| Single | Ignore | Ignore | Basic |
| Small | Ignore | Basic | Consider |
| Org | Sketch | Consider | Design for |
| Large | Note | Design for | Optimize |
| Internet | Note | Architecture | Primary concern |

Constraints {
  Don't optimize prematurely.
  But don't preclude scaling with bad interfaces.
  Simple code that could scale > scaled code you don't need.
}
